## Integration with Other Modules

This module becomes much more powerful when connected to the rest of your ERP:

* **Planning** → Planned quantities and activities feed the BOQ.
* **Procurement** → Purchase Orders consume the allocated budget.
* **Inventory** → Material issues reduce the remaining budget.
* **Finance** → Supplier invoices and client payments update project financials.
* **Site** → Daily progress updates feed into valuations and payment certificates.
* **Reports** → Management gets real-time Budget vs Actual and profitability reports.

---

For **CONSTRACTOR**, I would consider the **BOQ / QS module** one of the core pillars of the ERP, alongside **Projects, Procurement, Inventory, Finance, and Planning**. Once this module is complete and integrated, your system will resemble the workflow found in professional construction management platforms used by medium and large contractors.









This is actually where your ERP starts becoming a **decision-making system**, not just a record-keeping system.

A QS doesn't want to spend all day typing data. They want the system to **calculate, warn, compare, and automate** as much as possible.

